L1: Vitamin D status and its relation to exercise performance and iron status in young ice hockey players
L2: PLoS ONE (2018)
L3: Joanna Orysiak, Joanna Mazur-Rozycka, John Fitzgerald, Michal Starczewski, Jadwiga Malczewska-Lenczowska, Krzysztof Busko
L4: 1 
L5:  Department of Nutrition Physiology and Dietetics, Institute of Sport – National Research Institute, Warsaw, Poland
L6: 2 
L7:  Central Institute For Labour Protection – National Research Institute (CIOP- PIB), Department of Ergonomics, Warsaw, Poland
L8: 3 
L9:  Department of Kinesiology and Public Health Education, University of North Dakota, Grand Forks, North Dakota, United States of America
L10: 4 
L11:  Department of Physiology, Institute of Sport – National Research Institute, Warsaw, Poland
L12: 5 
L13:  Department of Anatomy and Biomechanics, Kazimierz Wielki University, Bydgoszcz, Poland
L14: Medical University of Gdańsk, POLAND
L15: Objectives
L16: The aim was to examine the association between serum vitamin D concentration and isometric strength of various muscle groups, vertical jump performance, and repeated sprint ability in young ice hockey players. The secondary aim was to determine the association between vitamin D deficiency and indices of iron status.
L17: Methods
L18: Fifty male ice hockey players (17.2±0.9 years) participated in this cross-sectional study. Exercise performance was evaluated using isometric strength measures of upper and lower extremities, vertical jump performance and repeated sprint ability (RSA). Blood samples were collected for the determination of serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D (25(OH)D) and multiple indicies of iron status.
L19: Results
L20: The mean serum 25(OH)D concentration was 30.4 ng·ml -1  and ranged from 12.5 to 91.4 ng·ml -1 . Eleven participants (22%) had vitamin D deficiency and 20 athletes (40%) had vitamin D insufficiency. Serum 25(OH)D concentration was not positively correlated with isometric muscle strength, vertical jump performance, or RSA after adjusting for age, training experience, fat mass, fat free mass and height. Serum 25(OH)D concentration was not associated with indices of iron status.
L21: Conclusion
L22: Vitamin D insufficiency is highly prevalent in ice hockey players, but 25(OH)D concentration but it is not associated with exercise performance or indices of iron status.
